      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Left ventricular (LV) function was evaluated in 31 patients, who had total occlusion of the left anterior descending coronary artery and less than 70% stenosis of the other two major coronary arteries or any branch. Fifteen of 31 patients had a history of angina pectoris before acute myocardial infarction (AMI) and 16 of 31 patients had no history of angina pectoris before AMI. The patients with angina pectoris before AMI had a significantly better ejection fraction, percentage of abnormally contracting segment, and regional wall motion than those without angina pectoris before AMI. These data suggest that the symptom of angina pectoris before AMI could be a favorable sign in preserving LV function when the patients subsequently had AMI."},
